00:01:03.363 --> 00:01:05.732
This is the parent weekend.

00:01:05.732 --> 00:01:07.567
So if we look at--

00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:09.269
why is that happening?

00:01:09.269 --> 00:01:12.939
If we look at the--

00:01:12.939 --> 00:01:16.910
oh, that's not good.

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:20.146
OK, there we go.

00:01:20.146 --> 00:01:25.385
If we look at the MIT family
weekend, there is activities.

00:01:25.385 --> 00:01:27.253
And so what I
thought is that what

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:28.388
we could do in this class--

00:01:28.388 --> 00:01:29.355
we've got to learn a little bit.

00:01:29.355 --> 00:01:31.324
We're going to learn
about x-rays today.

00:01:31.324 --> 00:01:33.560
But what I thought
we could do is also

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:37.764
add some ideas for
activities that you all

00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:40.033
could do with your kids.

00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:41.167
But it doesn't even matter.

00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:43.336
It could be with any kids.

00:01:43.336 --> 00:01:47.473
But just more activities to
do to give you more options.

00:01:47.473 --> 00:01:50.043
And so in particular, I've
got five suggestions for you

00:01:50.043 --> 00:01:51.878
that we'll talk about today.

00:01:51.878 --> 00:01:53.813
The first one is staying warm.

00:01:53.813 --> 00:01:55.315
It's gotten really
cold, so we're

00:01:55.315 --> 00:01:57.317
going to talk
about staying warm.

00:01:57.317 --> 00:01:59.185
And then the next
thing we'll do is

00:01:59.185 --> 00:02:02.155
we'll appreciate the
fall and all the colors.

00:02:02.155 --> 00:02:03.323
And we'll cook.

00:02:03.323 --> 00:02:05.325
Cooking is such a
wonderful thing to do.

00:02:05.325 --> 00:02:07.127
I hope you guys
can do it together.

00:02:07.127 --> 00:02:08.261
We'll talk about that.

00:02:08.261 --> 00:02:11.431
And we'll talk about phones and
how they crack their screens.

00:02:11.431 --> 00:02:13.800
And we have some sort
of larger versions

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.870
here that we'll talk about.

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:18.538
And of course, it
wouldn't be complete

00:02:18.538 --> 00:02:20.240
if we didn't blow
bubbles, because that's

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:24.644
a really fun activity no
matter what age you are.

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:26.079
So that's our plan.

00:02:26.079 --> 00:02:28.047
That's our plan today.

00:02:28.047 --> 00:02:29.949
Now let's get started.

00:02:29.949 --> 00:02:32.185
So let's start with
the first activity,

00:02:32.185 --> 00:02:33.119
and it's staying warm.

00:02:36.555 --> 00:02:42.061
So I love candles, and I think
a lot of us have seen candles.

00:02:42.061 --> 00:02:47.433
The difference is between
everyone else's and your kids

00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:49.067
who are in this
class who see candles

00:02:49.067 --> 00:02:50.136
is they don't see candles.

00:02:50.136 --> 00:02:54.274
They see C25 H52 combusting.

00:02:54.274 --> 00:02:57.143
And your kids now, when
they see a candle--

00:02:57.143 --> 00:02:59.879
if you guys go out to dinner and
there's a candle on the table,

00:02:59.879 --> 00:03:00.346
the first thing--

00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:01.948
I guarantee you the
first thing they're

00:03:01.948 --> 00:03:04.651
going to do is assess
the volume of the room,

00:03:04.651 --> 00:03:08.087
figure out how much oxygen in
grams there is in that room,

00:03:08.087 --> 00:03:09.489
and they're going
to decide which

00:03:09.489 --> 00:03:13.293
one is the limiting
reagent, the candle or you.

00:03:13.293 --> 00:03:14.761
And they'll make a choice.

00:03:14.761 --> 00:03:17.897
They may get you out of the
room because you don't know.

00:03:17.897 --> 00:03:18.898
You've got to know that.

00:03:18.898 --> 00:03:21.434
And of course, they'll have
their periodic tables with them

00:03:21.434 --> 00:03:22.535
at the dinner.

00:03:22.535 --> 00:03:25.872
Don't be surprised
when they take it out.

00:03:25.872 --> 00:03:28.241
Now, your students-- oh,
yeah, that's perfect.

00:03:28.241 --> 00:03:30.043
Your students also know--

00:03:30.043 --> 00:03:31.811
your kids, my
students, your kids--

00:03:31.811 --> 00:03:36.049
also know that a lot of the
earliest chemistry, going

00:03:36.049 --> 00:03:39.819
all the way back to the times
of Democritus thousands of years

00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:42.855
ago, the way it started
was because people

00:03:42.855 --> 00:03:46.926
were breaking things and
smashing things and lighting

00:03:46.926 --> 00:03:48.127
stuff on fire.

00:03:48.127 --> 00:03:50.363
And we've got the whole
periodic table in this class,

00:03:50.363 --> 00:03:54.934
but we still know that the most
fun element of all to light

00:03:54.934 --> 00:03:56.603
on fire is the very first.

00:03:56.603 --> 00:03:57.670
It's hydrogen.

00:03:57.670 --> 00:04:01.040
And so I thought I would
show you what that's like.

00:04:02.008 --> 00:04:02.609
OK.

00:04:02.609 --> 00:04:04.677
So here's hydrogen.

00:04:04.677 --> 00:04:09.148
And like I said, this is also--

00:04:09.148 --> 00:04:11.150
it's a way to think about--

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:12.919
oh, that's a beautiful thing.

00:04:12.919 --> 00:04:14.254
Is that a beautiful thing?

00:04:14.254 --> 00:04:16.723
I've got to do it again.

00:04:16.723 --> 00:04:19.559
And as your kids know,
I can do this a lot.

00:04:19.559 --> 00:04:22.428
But it's just-- and
my hand doesn't--

00:04:22.428 --> 00:04:24.297
I don't feel it because
of the heat capacity.

00:04:24.297 --> 00:04:25.632
But that's another class.

00:04:25.632 --> 00:04:30.270
But what we're interested
in is combustion.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:32.272
So there's that one there, too.

00:04:32.272 --> 00:04:37.543
So this is all about the context
of staying warm, of course.

00:04:37.543 --> 00:04:40.580
You don't need to light
hydrogen on fire to stay warm.

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:44.150
But I also have suggested
discussion topics.

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:46.486
Because I don't want you
just to do these activities.

00:04:46.486 --> 00:04:47.553
I want you to be engaged.

00:04:47.553 --> 00:04:49.956
It might have been a while
since you guys have connected--

00:04:49.956 --> 00:04:52.792
I don't know-- in person.

00:04:52.792 --> 00:04:56.229
And so here, maybe you don't
know what to say at first.

00:04:56.229 --> 00:05:00.500
And so for example, you could
talk about balancing chemical

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:01.834
reactions.

00:05:01.834 --> 00:05:05.171
You could talk about
the concept of a mole.

00:05:05.171 --> 00:05:06.873
And you could also
talk about limiting

00:05:06.873 --> 00:05:10.376
reagents, which are really,
really important in life.

00:05:10.376 --> 00:05:13.413
So that's my first
suggested activity.

00:05:13.413 --> 00:05:15.882
Like I said, we'll have
five new activities.

00:05:15.882 --> 00:05:18.818
That's the first one.

00:05:18.818 --> 00:05:21.220
But like I also said, we've
got to learn something today.

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:24.557
And students, this is not
on the exam, as a reminder.

00:05:24.557 --> 00:05:27.060
But we do need to
learn what X-rays are.

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:30.029
Because next week, we're going
to be shining them on stuff.

00:05:30.029 --> 00:05:34.200
So today, we're going to learn
about how X-rays are generated.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:36.536
And it all starts with
the very first selfie.

00:05:36.536 --> 00:05:37.070
This is it.

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:39.639
Does anybody know who that is?

00:05:39.639 --> 00:05:45.445
That is Anna Rontgen. That is
the wife of Wilhelm Ronten.

00:05:45.445 --> 00:05:48.281
That's the very first
X-ray ever taken.

00:05:48.281 --> 00:05:49.982
That's the very first
X-ray ever taken.

00:05:49.982 --> 00:05:56.356
And Wilhelm Rontgen was playing
around with cathode ray tubes.

00:05:56.356 --> 00:05:57.390
And so he--

00:05:57.390 --> 00:05:58.491
I love this quote of him.

00:05:58.491 --> 00:06:00.960
He said, I did not think.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:02.295
I investigated.

00:06:02.295 --> 00:06:03.796
And that's-- now, we think.

00:06:03.796 --> 00:06:05.365
Of course we think.

00:06:05.365 --> 00:06:07.467
But what we do in this
class is we investigate.

00:06:07.467 --> 00:06:08.901
We're explorers.

00:06:08.901 --> 00:06:10.970
Were explorers of
chemistry and how chemistry

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:15.742
connects to what we see in life
and what we're doing every day.

00:06:15.742 --> 00:06:17.910
And so how did Rontgen do this?

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:18.678
OK.

00:06:18.678 --> 00:06:27.186
Well, literally, it was a dark
and stormy night, literally.

00:06:27.186 --> 00:06:29.889
And he was in the
basement in his lab.

00:06:29.889 --> 00:06:31.157
That's him.

00:06:31.157 --> 00:06:33.926
And it's going to be stormy
tomorrow, so you guys could

00:06:33.926 --> 00:06:38.398
even try to think about how
it felt. And so what he did

00:06:38.398 --> 00:06:39.165
is he--

00:06:39.165 --> 00:06:42.168
everybody was playing with
these cathode ray tubes.

00:06:42.168 --> 00:06:45.004
We learned a lot about
cathode ray tubes.

00:06:45.004 --> 00:06:49.041
So here, you put some
kind of cathode here.

00:06:49.041 --> 00:06:51.244
And then there is an anode
here, and you hook it up

00:06:51.244 --> 00:06:53.312
to a voltage.

00:06:53.312 --> 00:06:57.183
And what happens
is electrons fly.

00:06:57.183 --> 00:07:01.087
This is a cathode ray
tube, so it's the CRT.

00:07:01.087 --> 00:07:04.757
Electrons will fly off of
the cathode and go here.

00:07:04.757 --> 00:07:07.126
Now, if they go
through the anode,

00:07:07.126 --> 00:07:09.695
and then you put a
screen here, those

00:07:09.695 --> 00:07:12.165
are the very first cathode
ray tube experiments.

00:07:12.165 --> 00:07:14.200
And we talked about how
this led to the discovery

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:16.102
of the electron itself.

00:07:16.102 --> 00:07:19.172
But what Rontgen did is
he said, now, hold on.

00:07:19.172 --> 00:07:20.573
What if I changed--

00:07:20.573 --> 00:07:21.474
I put a voltage here.

00:07:21.474 --> 00:07:23.075
What if I amp that up?

00:07:23.075 --> 00:07:25.711
What if I just keep going?

00:07:25.711 --> 00:07:28.915
Oh, by the way, there is
a vacuum inside of here.

00:07:28.915 --> 00:07:30.049
There has to be a vacuum.

00:07:30.049 --> 00:07:31.918
And if I am the voltage
up-- you know what?

00:07:31.918 --> 00:07:38.491
Actually, I'm going to put
a metal here for the anode.

00:07:38.491 --> 00:07:40.426
I'm going to make that
some kind of metal,

00:07:40.426 --> 00:07:43.663
and I'm going to hit the
metal with the electrons.

00:07:43.663 --> 00:07:45.264
Now, this is exactly
what happened.

00:07:45.264 --> 00:07:50.570
What happened is
the room glowed.

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:51.871
The room glowed.

00:07:51.871 --> 00:07:55.808
So Rontgen had-- showing you
there, the lights are off.

00:07:55.808 --> 00:07:59.111
The lights are off,
but the room was lit.

00:07:59.111 --> 00:08:00.279
And he said, oh my goodness.

00:08:00.279 --> 00:08:02.014
What is going on here?

00:08:02.014 --> 00:08:03.649
What is going on?

00:08:03.649 --> 00:08:05.485
And what he decided--

00:08:05.485 --> 00:08:08.454
he literally
discovered a new type

00:08:08.454 --> 00:08:11.190
of ray, a new type of light ray.

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:13.759
And because he didn't
know what to call it--

00:08:13.759 --> 00:08:16.929
he didn't have a name on
that dark and stormy night--

00:08:16.929 --> 00:08:22.034
he wrote down the letter x,
which he regretted later.

00:08:22.034 --> 00:08:23.102
He really did.

00:08:23.102 --> 00:08:24.704
Because everybody
started calling it--

00:08:24.704 --> 00:08:26.672
he didn't know what to
call it, so he called it

00:08:26.672 --> 00:08:30.843
an X-ray, which is
what happens when

00:08:30.843 --> 00:08:33.145
you do this experiment
with a very high voltage.

00:08:33.145 --> 00:08:39.018
You get rays that came off of
this and illuminated the room.

00:08:39.018 --> 00:08:40.786
And he said, I don't
know what to call it.

00:08:40.786 --> 00:08:42.855
I'll put the letter x for now.

00:08:42.855 --> 00:08:46.092
And that stuck.

00:08:46.092 --> 00:08:48.060
Now, these X-rays are unique.

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:51.063
They're unique because
they have very high energy.

00:08:51.063 --> 00:08:52.198
They have very high energy.

00:08:52.198 --> 00:08:54.000
So the X-ray energies--

00:08:54.000 --> 00:09:04.243
the energies are in the
range of 100 eV to 100 KeV.

00:09:04.243 --> 00:09:06.379
That's a big energy.

00:09:06.379 --> 00:09:07.880
But we know how to
go back and forth

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:09.181
between energy and wavelengths.

00:09:09.181 --> 00:09:13.085
So we know that that means
that the wavelength range is

00:09:13.085 --> 00:09:16.856
between 0.01 and 10 nanometers.

00:09:16.856 --> 00:09:18.624
And that's what's
really important.

00:09:18.624 --> 00:09:24.230
You came up with a source that
has wavelengths in this very,

00:09:24.230 --> 00:09:26.532
very special regime.

00:09:26.532 --> 00:09:28.334
This is a very special
regime because it's

00:09:28.334 --> 00:09:32.138
the distance between atoms.

00:09:32.138 --> 00:09:34.674
Now, there's two
types of x-rays.

00:09:34.674 --> 00:09:42.048
But first, let's-- at the
time, you might think, people--

00:09:42.048 --> 00:09:46.152
why wasn't he wearing
anything, like at the dentist?

00:09:46.152 --> 00:09:47.620
Why wasn't he wearing anything?

00:09:47.620 --> 00:09:51.724
They didn't know that
radiation could be dangerous.

00:09:51.724 --> 00:09:54.026
And so if you look
at the time, there

00:09:54.026 --> 00:09:57.863
were ads like this for a
system to remove the hair.

00:09:57.863 --> 00:10:01.634
And it was advertised as
effective, safe, and painless.

00:10:01.634 --> 00:10:06.405
This is-- those are X-rays, one
of the first uses of X-rays.

00:10:06.405 --> 00:10:10.710
This is a token to
get into a bath that's

00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:13.546
called a radium bath.

00:10:13.546 --> 00:10:17.750
It's a water bath where you have
radium that you either drink

00:10:17.750 --> 00:10:21.253
or is in the water itself.

00:10:21.253 --> 00:10:24.757
And look at what was
happening in Paris.

00:10:24.757 --> 00:10:27.393
Radium cure, a fad
of Paris society.

00:10:27.393 --> 00:10:30.763
Patients play bridge and take
tea for two hours daily--

00:10:30.763 --> 00:10:32.131
daily-- in a drawing room.

00:10:32.131 --> 00:10:33.599
This is what it says.

00:10:33.599 --> 00:10:34.333
December 9th.

00:10:34.333 --> 00:10:37.203
The afternoon radium cue is the
latest craze in Paris society.

00:10:37.203 --> 00:10:40.339
The popularity of the
treatment, new to Paris,

00:10:40.339 --> 00:10:43.509
has devolved quite suddenly and
is due, no doubt, to the fact

00:10:43.509 --> 00:10:47.046
that it is exceedingly pleasant.

00:10:47.046 --> 00:10:48.881
Oh, France.

00:10:48.881 --> 00:10:51.517
Right?

00:10:51.517 --> 00:10:52.551
They didn't know.

00:10:52.551 --> 00:10:53.352
They didn't know.

00:10:53.352 --> 00:10:54.920
And it was the same
with these X-rays.

00:10:54.920 --> 00:10:56.756
What is this thing?

00:10:56.756 --> 00:11:00.126
Well, it turned out it was
a very, very important type

00:11:00.126 --> 00:11:00.893
of light.

00:11:00.893 --> 00:11:03.062
And because of its
importance, Ronten

00:11:03.062 --> 00:11:06.899
won the first ever Nobel
Prize given in physics.

00:11:06.899 --> 00:11:11.137
There is an element, number
111, named Roentgenium.

00:11:11.137 --> 00:11:14.740
And 60 years later, the
DNA was discovered--

00:11:14.740 --> 00:11:17.376
the structure of the
DNA, the double helix

00:11:17.376 --> 00:11:19.812
was discovered
because of X-rays.

00:11:19.812 --> 00:11:24.083
So the impact of
X-rays is tremendous.

00:11:24.083 --> 00:11:26.886
Now, the next activity,
as I mentioned,

00:11:26.886 --> 00:11:30.222
has to do with
enjoying the fall.

00:11:30.222 --> 00:11:35.027
Now, the thing is that New
England has beautiful colors.

00:11:35.027 --> 00:11:37.963
And so if you haven't taken
a stroll around the parks,

00:11:37.963 --> 00:11:41.100
or maybe you go up
to a nice hike--

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:44.303
you'll see these beautiful
colors changing in the leaves.

00:11:44.303 --> 00:11:47.139
But the thing is that
in our class, your kids,

00:11:47.139 --> 00:11:50.276
they don't see that anymore.

00:11:50.276 --> 00:11:51.277
No.

00:11:51.277 --> 00:11:51.777
They don't.

00:11:51.777 --> 00:11:53.746
Because when they
see colors, they

00:11:53.746 --> 00:11:56.248
think about electron levels.

00:11:56.248 --> 00:11:57.450
That's what they think about.

00:11:57.450 --> 00:11:58.784
They can't help it.

00:11:58.784 --> 00:11:59.785
They cannot help it.

00:11:59.785 --> 00:12:03.055
So when they see orange or
red, all they're thinking about

00:12:03.055 --> 00:12:05.458
is, oh, my goodness, there
is an electron level here,

00:12:05.458 --> 00:12:07.993
and an electron getting excited,
and it's coming back down

00:12:07.993 --> 00:12:11.964
and emitting a photon, and
how those levels are changing

00:12:11.964 --> 00:12:14.467
as you go from one
leaf to another,

00:12:14.467 --> 00:12:18.771
and how chemistry is
at the heart of it all.

00:12:18.771 --> 00:12:20.873
Chemistry is at the
heart of it all.

00:12:20.873 --> 00:12:22.508
And so that's what
they're thinking of.

00:12:22.508 --> 00:12:23.843
And we can use chemistry.

00:12:23.843 --> 00:12:27.913
I don't know if we have
the next one ready here.

00:12:27.913 --> 00:12:34.754
We can use chemistry not only to
change the levels of electrons

00:12:34.754 --> 00:12:37.790
that are in this material, which
then changes the color of light

00:12:37.790 --> 00:12:39.225
that comes off of it--

00:12:39.225 --> 00:12:40.993
it's all about the electrons--

00:12:40.993 --> 00:12:43.095
but we can use
chemistry to control

00:12:43.095 --> 00:12:45.698
how quickly that happens.

00:12:45.698 --> 00:12:48.434
Nature does it on this
yearly time scale.

00:12:48.434 --> 00:12:52.538
We can do it on any time scale
because of chemical kinetics.

00:12:52.538 --> 00:12:54.673
So we're going to get
that experiment out here

00:12:54.673 --> 00:12:56.075
in a second.

00:12:56.075 --> 00:12:59.278
Meanwhile-- oh, there it is.

00:12:59.278 --> 00:13:01.013
Can we give the
helpers a huge hand?

00:13:01.013 --> 00:13:03.082
Because they are--
thank you, guys.

00:13:03.082 --> 00:13:04.984
