00:16:12.805 --> 00:16:16.375
So that's what I want
to say about the stroll.

00:16:16.375 --> 00:16:17.576
You might wind up down there.

00:16:17.576 --> 00:16:19.478
I don't know.

00:16:19.478 --> 00:16:22.881
OK, back to our X-rays.

00:16:22.881 --> 00:16:25.217
Where were we?

00:16:25.217 --> 00:16:27.152
So x-rays are coming
off of this metal.

00:16:27.152 --> 00:16:28.354
It's super high voltage.

00:16:28.354 --> 00:16:29.888
It's a a dark and stormy night.

00:16:29.888 --> 00:16:34.259
What happened next
is that we had

00:16:34.259 --> 00:16:39.264
to figure out what were the
properties of this light.

00:16:39.264 --> 00:16:41.200
And what we really
mean are things

00:16:41.200 --> 00:16:43.068
like intensity and wavelength.

00:16:43.068 --> 00:16:46.805
Now, I wrote down ranges there.

00:16:46.805 --> 00:16:51.844
But basically, what you have
with X-rays are two types.

00:16:51.844 --> 00:17:00.052
You have type 1, which
is called continuous.

00:17:00.052 --> 00:17:06.558
And it arises continuous--

00:17:06.558 --> 00:17:07.192
continuous.

00:17:07.192 --> 00:17:09.194
And it actually-- this
is a pretty good name,

00:17:09.194 --> 00:17:13.932
because it's a continuous
energy up to a point.

00:17:13.932 --> 00:17:15.501
It's a continuous spectrum.

00:17:15.501 --> 00:17:16.568
So what do I mean by that?

00:17:16.568 --> 00:17:18.537
Well, what I mean by
that-- to understand that,

00:17:18.537 --> 00:17:20.873
we have to know how we get it.

00:17:20.873 --> 00:17:22.907
There are two types of X-rays.

00:17:22.907 --> 00:17:24.810
In this first type,
what we have is

00:17:24.810 --> 00:17:28.079
we have some atoms with a
bunch of protons in there.

00:17:28.079 --> 00:17:29.915
This is like that metal atom.

00:17:29.915 --> 00:17:33.218
And I've shot really
high-energy electrons at it.

00:17:33.218 --> 00:17:36.989
But that atom has electrons
sitting around it.

00:17:36.989 --> 00:17:37.689
We know that.

00:17:37.689 --> 00:17:41.326
1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8.

00:17:41.326 --> 00:17:42.661
OK, and then again.

00:17:42.661 --> 00:17:44.930
And these are the
quantum levels.

00:17:44.930 --> 00:17:49.501
This would be n
equals 3, n equals 2.

00:17:49.501 --> 00:17:52.738
And in here, we've
got n equals 1.

00:17:52.738 --> 00:17:53.806
But now, here's the thing.

00:17:53.806 --> 00:17:57.776
I'm shooting high-energy
electrons at this atom.

00:17:57.776 --> 00:18:01.580
So one of the first things that
can happen is nothing at all.

00:18:01.580 --> 00:18:05.317
So if I shoot it, say,
up here, way away--

00:18:05.317 --> 00:18:08.320
if it's all the way up here
going way away from the atom

00:18:08.320 --> 00:18:12.991
and doesn't feel it, then
it might just come through.

00:18:12.991 --> 00:18:15.794
It might have some
energy-- call it e0--

00:18:15.794 --> 00:18:17.329
and it just came
all the way through.

00:18:17.329 --> 00:18:18.864
It didn't interact.

00:18:18.864 --> 00:18:20.232
Well, that's kind of boring.

00:18:20.232 --> 00:18:25.104
What if I interacted-- what if
I'm out here, and I interact--

00:18:25.104 --> 00:18:28.040
I got through some of
those outer electrons.

00:18:28.040 --> 00:18:29.475
Immediately, what do I see?

00:18:29.475 --> 00:18:33.445
If I'm a negative charge,
I see this positive charge,

00:18:33.445 --> 00:18:36.181
and I'm like, ah,
that looks good.

00:18:36.181 --> 00:18:38.016
It's an attraction.

00:18:38.016 --> 00:18:43.222
And so the electron
curves like that.

00:18:43.222 --> 00:18:45.891
Now, that means
that it accelerated.

00:18:45.891 --> 00:18:48.994
This is not a electron
stuck in an orbit.

00:18:48.994 --> 00:18:49.628
No.

00:18:49.628 --> 00:18:52.664
This one was free,
and it remained free.

00:18:52.664 --> 00:18:55.834
But because it got inside of
the electron cloud of the atom,

00:18:55.834 --> 00:18:57.202
it saw a a positive charge.

00:18:57.202 --> 00:18:59.138
And that bends it, and
that accelerates it.

00:18:59.138 --> 00:19:03.475
And when something that's
charged accelerates,

00:19:03.475 --> 00:19:05.811
it gives off a photon.

00:19:05.811 --> 00:19:08.313
It gives off a photon.

00:19:08.313 --> 00:19:11.483
So that might be
some kind of energy

00:19:11.483 --> 00:19:16.722
that it has left
over that we call e1.

00:19:16.722 --> 00:19:20.025
And this would be
like the photon 1.

00:19:20.025 --> 00:19:23.495
And we know that it's
lost energy to the photon.

00:19:23.495 --> 00:19:24.763
It's decelerated.

00:19:24.763 --> 00:19:27.499
It's decelerated.

00:19:27.499 --> 00:19:31.270
So we know that e0
is greater than e1.

00:19:31.270 --> 00:19:35.474
But I could have shot
another one through,

00:19:35.474 --> 00:19:38.510
and this one got even luckier.

00:19:38.510 --> 00:19:41.513
These are my electrons,
incoming electrons.

00:19:41.513 --> 00:19:44.683
This one got even closer
to the positive charge,

00:19:44.683 --> 00:19:46.919
felt an even
stronger attraction,

00:19:46.919 --> 00:19:48.520
and decelerated even more.

00:19:52.257 --> 00:19:57.863
And so it also emits a photon.

00:19:57.863 --> 00:20:01.433
And so we know that e2
must be smaller than e1.

00:20:01.433 --> 00:20:04.469
It's left with less energy.

00:20:04.469 --> 00:20:07.739
Now, what you're doing is
you're decelerating electrons

00:20:07.739 --> 00:20:12.544
by having them pass through
the cloud of charge of an atom.

00:20:12.544 --> 00:20:14.780
And that deceleration
emits photons.

00:20:14.780 --> 00:20:16.481
And you can see right
away what I'm doing

00:20:16.481 --> 00:20:19.351
is I'm breaking the electrons.

00:20:19.351 --> 00:20:23.121
Well, that's exactly what this
kind of radiation is called.

00:20:23.121 --> 00:20:28.994
This radiation is, in fact,
called Bremsstrahlung.

00:20:37.102 --> 00:20:42.474
And that literally
means braking radiation.

00:20:42.474 --> 00:20:44.676
Braking radiation.

00:20:48.914 --> 00:20:51.550
It means breaking.

00:20:51.550 --> 00:20:54.219
Well, if I take this
very simple picture,

00:20:54.219 --> 00:20:58.423
and I try to make a plot-- like
I said, if I want to understand

00:20:58.423 --> 00:21:00.492
my light, I've got
to know about how

00:21:00.492 --> 00:21:04.429
it behaves as a function of,
say, wavelength or frequency.

00:21:04.429 --> 00:21:11.370
And so if you plotted the
wavelength with the intensity,

00:21:11.370 --> 00:21:13.639
well, you can see right
here I'm going to get--

00:21:13.639 --> 00:21:17.276
OK, so I'm going to
get some photon that

00:21:17.276 --> 00:21:20.779
comes out that has
some lambda, and then

00:21:20.779 --> 00:21:23.248
another photon that comes
out that has another lambda.

00:21:23.248 --> 00:21:27.352
But you can see that that
electron could have been

00:21:27.352 --> 00:21:31.923
braked at any loss of energy.

00:21:31.923 --> 00:21:37.729
And so what you get is something
that is very continuous

00:21:37.729 --> 00:21:39.331
up to a point.

00:21:39.331 --> 00:21:41.133
This is the shape
that it looks like.

00:21:41.133 --> 00:21:43.602
So I can get-- this is lambda.

00:21:43.602 --> 00:21:46.071
So if lambda is out
there, this would

00:21:46.071 --> 00:21:50.776
be like lower energy photons.

00:21:50.776 --> 00:21:55.914
And up here, it's higher,
higher-energy photons.

00:21:59.985 --> 00:22:01.353
Now, these are all x-rays.

00:22:01.353 --> 00:22:05.223
They're in this very
high-energy range.

00:22:05.223 --> 00:22:07.359
But you've got this
continuous spectrum.

00:22:07.359 --> 00:22:09.561
Because the electron could
have been slowed down

00:22:09.561 --> 00:22:14.166
in any way except
higher than this point.

00:22:14.166 --> 00:22:15.400
Why?

00:22:15.400 --> 00:22:18.737
Well, that's just determined
by how much energy

00:22:18.737 --> 00:22:19.971
that initial electron got.

00:22:19.971 --> 00:22:25.177
Because you can imagine this
point is a special point.

00:22:25.177 --> 00:22:32.584
This represents a fully
stopped electron, fully braked,

00:22:32.584 --> 00:22:34.419
fully stopped electron.

00:22:34.419 --> 00:22:39.224
So that incoming electron has
transferred all of its energy

00:22:39.224 --> 00:22:40.225
to the photon.

00:22:40.225 --> 00:22:42.327
And because this
is higher energy,

00:22:42.327 --> 00:22:43.795
that's as high as it gets.

00:22:43.795 --> 00:22:46.131
That makes sense, right?

00:22:46.131 --> 00:22:46.765
OK.

00:22:46.765 --> 00:22:48.200
We have a name for that too.

00:22:48.200 --> 00:22:49.968
That's the Duane Hunt limit.

00:22:55.607 --> 00:22:59.511
And basically, it says
that the energy of--

00:22:59.511 --> 00:23:08.153
the maximum energy, e max of
the photon that can come out

00:23:08.153 --> 00:23:13.024
is equal to the electron
times the voltage, the charge

00:23:13.024 --> 00:23:15.961
of the electron times
the voltage across, ev

00:23:15.961 --> 00:23:22.768
across the tube, and that that
is equal to hc over lambda min.

00:23:22.768 --> 00:23:26.405
This is just saying max
energy equals hc over min

00:23:26.405 --> 00:23:31.543
wavelength, energy wavelength.

00:23:31.543 --> 00:23:32.611
So that's one type.

00:23:32.611 --> 00:23:33.378
And if I had--

00:23:33.378 --> 00:23:35.480
but now you can see
if I shot it, now I'm

00:23:35.480 --> 00:23:37.315
bumping up the
voltage even more,

00:23:37.315 --> 00:23:40.018
and I'm shooting it with
higher-energy electrons.

00:23:40.018 --> 00:23:43.989
Well, you can imagine that
if the electrons I shoot in

00:23:43.989 --> 00:23:47.559
have a higher energy,
then that point is going

00:23:47.559 --> 00:23:52.397
to go in and in like that.

00:23:52.397 --> 00:23:56.134
So this would be
Bremsstrahlung radiation,

00:23:56.134 --> 00:24:02.641
and this would be
increasing incident-- inc?

00:24:02.641 --> 00:24:03.742
That's not good.

00:24:03.742 --> 00:24:08.780
Incident electron energy.

00:24:08.780 --> 00:24:15.520
And I will put a box around
this so that it's increasing,

00:24:15.520 --> 00:24:17.489
that arrow going like that.

00:24:17.489 --> 00:24:20.058
So if I shoot it with higher
and higher-energy electrons,

00:24:20.058 --> 00:24:22.761
I'm still going to
have a minimum lambda,

00:24:22.761 --> 00:24:24.496
but it's going to get
smaller and smaller.

00:24:24.496 --> 00:24:27.232
Continuous x-rays.

00:24:27.232 --> 00:24:27.833
OK.

00:24:27.833 --> 00:24:29.401
That's type 1.

00:24:29.401 --> 00:24:32.971
Now, speaking of breaking--

00:24:32.971 --> 00:24:35.707
now, speaking of breaking--

00:24:35.707 --> 00:24:36.208
OK.

00:24:36.208 --> 00:24:40.111
Now, here is my third
activity, and it's cooking.

00:24:40.111 --> 00:24:41.880
And I love cooking,
and I hope you do too.

00:24:41.880 --> 00:24:43.448
It's a really fun thing to do.

00:24:43.448 --> 00:24:46.952
And I think that I love
cooking with cast iron.

00:24:46.952 --> 00:24:50.689
So maybe you've got a piece
of cast iron in your dorm

00:24:50.689 --> 00:24:53.959
that you can pull out,
and pull out a recipe,

00:24:53.959 --> 00:24:55.927
and start making something.

00:24:55.927 --> 00:24:57.529
But what I want you
to think about--

00:24:57.529 --> 00:24:59.464
and this is the
thing-- is your kids,

00:24:59.464 --> 00:25:02.167
our students don't see
it as cast iron anymore.

00:25:02.167 --> 00:25:05.704
They see it as iron with
interstitially doped carbon.

00:25:05.704 --> 00:25:08.773
That's what they see it as.

00:25:08.773 --> 00:25:11.510
And so I want you to
see it that way too.

00:25:11.510 --> 00:25:13.278
And then that gets you thinking.

00:25:13.278 --> 00:25:15.447
How is this cast iron so strong?

00:25:15.447 --> 00:25:17.115
And oh, wait, the
early chemists--

00:25:17.115 --> 00:25:21.253
I heard they smashed and
they lit things on fire.

00:25:21.253 --> 00:25:22.821
How could we break
this cast iron?

00:25:22.821 --> 00:25:24.189
Could it break?

00:25:24.189 --> 00:25:25.590
Could it break?

00:25:25.590 --> 00:25:27.425
Should we be worried about it?

00:25:27.425 --> 00:25:30.295
And so what I think you should
do-- oh, yeah, there it is.

00:25:30.295 --> 00:25:31.696
Let's take this.

00:25:31.696 --> 00:25:32.831
You take the--

00:25:32.831 --> 00:25:34.799
I don't have a pan,
but I have this.

00:25:34.799 --> 00:25:36.034
Is this filled?

00:25:36.034 --> 00:25:37.836
OK.

00:25:37.836 --> 00:25:39.371
And you take out a
hammer because you

00:25:39.371 --> 00:25:42.107
have a hammer in the closet.

00:25:42.107 --> 00:25:44.075
And you go like this.

00:25:44.075 --> 00:25:46.311
And nothing happens.

00:25:46.311 --> 00:25:48.880
And then you do it again.

00:25:48.880 --> 00:25:51.182
You say, well, you know what?

00:25:51.182 --> 00:25:53.518
Maybe we need to
go a little bigger.

00:25:53.518 --> 00:25:58.590
And you go into your closet,
and you find a sledgehammer.

00:25:58.590 --> 00:26:00.292
And you say, you know what?

00:26:00.292 --> 00:26:04.529
I think if we just use
this handy sledgehammer--

00:26:04.529 --> 00:26:07.299
hopefully not in your dorm--

00:26:07.299 --> 00:26:08.366
then maybe we'll break it.

00:26:08.366 --> 00:26:11.436
And so you keep hitting--

00:26:11.436 --> 00:26:13.838
not over the electrical.

00:26:13.838 --> 00:26:16.474
And you hit it again.

00:26:16.474 --> 00:26:18.577
And I won't use--

00:26:18.577 --> 00:26:21.112
I won't go extreme here,
but I will tell you

00:26:21.112 --> 00:26:24.082
that I've done it before.

00:26:24.082 --> 00:26:25.317
And nothing happens.

00:26:25.317 --> 00:26:26.084
Nothing happens.

00:26:26.084 --> 00:26:27.452
There's not even
a scratch on it.

00:26:30.655 --> 00:26:36.161
But these are your kids who
go to MIT, not another school.

00:26:36.161 --> 00:26:41.366
And they know-- and they know
that intermolecular forces

00:26:41.366 --> 00:26:42.934
have power.

00:26:42.934 --> 00:26:44.202
They've got a lot of power.

00:26:44.202 --> 00:26:45.370
And they know about bonding.

00:26:45.370 --> 00:26:47.372
And they know about
the hydrogen bond,

00:26:47.372 --> 00:26:50.475
and the power of the hydrogen
bond, and how strong it is,

00:26:50.475 --> 00:26:51.543
and why it is.

00:26:51.543 --> 00:26:53.812
And then they start
thinking about water.

00:26:53.812 --> 00:26:57.983
I wonder if we could leverage
the power of the hydrogen bond.

00:26:57.983 --> 00:27:00.385
Now, do we have liquid nitrogen?

00:27:00.385 --> 00:27:01.553
We do.

00:27:01.553 --> 00:27:02.053
OK.

00:27:04.723 --> 00:27:07.225
So they put it
together that we might

00:27:07.225 --> 00:27:09.894
be able to leverage that power
of the hydrogen. They knew.

00:27:09.894 --> 00:27:10.829
They saw the gecko.

00:27:10.829 --> 00:27:14.633
They say, the London dispersion
lets a gecko climb a wall.

00:27:14.633 --> 00:27:16.401
Well, then hydrogen
bonds might be what it

00:27:16.401 --> 00:27:19.237
