00:27:49.868 --> 00:27:50.735
OK.

00:27:50.735 --> 00:27:52.504
Well, let's see how this goes.

00:27:52.504 --> 00:27:54.105
And we might take a--

00:27:54.105 --> 00:27:56.708
we'll see what happens.

00:27:56.708 --> 00:28:02.213
Again, this is a bonding moment.

00:28:02.213 --> 00:28:04.983
This is a hydrogen
bonding moment.

00:28:04.983 --> 00:28:07.118
But this is where
the two of you--

00:28:07.118 --> 00:28:09.654
the three of you, the four of
you-- it could be a huge group.

00:28:09.654 --> 00:28:13.425
This is where you talk about
things like the crystal

00:28:13.425 --> 00:28:15.627
structure of iron.

00:28:15.627 --> 00:28:17.228
This is where you
talk about things

00:28:17.228 --> 00:28:18.697
like intermolecular forces.

00:28:18.697 --> 00:28:20.098
And we've learned them all.

00:28:20.098 --> 00:28:22.267
We learned about them
all in this class.

00:28:22.267 --> 00:28:25.704
Where you talk about the power
bonds and the power of the bond

00:28:25.704 --> 00:28:29.574
between you as a family.

00:28:29.574 --> 00:28:30.308
This is it.

00:28:30.308 --> 00:28:35.013
Just do it on this side
of the blast shield.

00:28:35.013 --> 00:28:35.580
OK.

00:28:35.580 --> 00:28:36.581
Now we'll let that sit.

00:28:36.581 --> 00:28:39.884
We'll see how that goes.

00:28:39.884 --> 00:28:42.487
And OK, yeah.

00:28:42.487 --> 00:28:43.221
That's good.

00:28:43.221 --> 00:28:45.123
Now, where were we?

00:28:45.123 --> 00:28:47.525
Oh, right.

00:28:47.525 --> 00:28:49.127
Oh, there's another
type of X-ray.

00:28:49.127 --> 00:28:51.062
There's another type of X-ray.

00:28:51.062 --> 00:28:55.066
And the other type of X-ray--

00:28:55.066 --> 00:28:56.568
that's the only board left.

00:29:02.273 --> 00:29:11.449
The other type of X-ray is from
a different phenomenon that

00:29:11.449 --> 00:29:15.854
also has to do with--

00:29:15.854 --> 00:29:20.592
it also has to do with
the atom and the electron.

00:29:20.592 --> 00:29:23.394
Because the one type is because
the electrons are coming

00:29:23.394 --> 00:29:25.130
through this cloud,
and they get a little

00:29:25.130 --> 00:29:27.398
attracted, and they lose
energy, and they give that off

00:29:27.398 --> 00:29:28.299
to an X-ray photon.

00:29:28.299 --> 00:29:31.936
But see, the other kind comes
from a direct excitation,

00:29:31.936 --> 00:29:35.774
the same kind that
we learned about when

00:29:35.774 --> 00:29:38.076
we looked at the Bohr atom.

00:29:38.076 --> 00:29:39.477
And so this is drawn here.

00:29:39.477 --> 00:29:41.513
Now, there's a very
important thing that happens.

00:29:41.513 --> 00:29:43.014
As we've learned
in this class, when

00:29:43.014 --> 00:29:45.517
you go from one
area of expertise

00:29:45.517 --> 00:29:47.385
to another-- like
crystallographers

00:29:47.385 --> 00:29:48.353
don't like commas.

00:29:48.353 --> 00:29:49.721
They don't like commas.

00:29:49.721 --> 00:29:51.356
Don't upset a crystallographer.

00:29:51.356 --> 00:29:52.223
It makes no sense.

00:29:52.223 --> 00:29:54.159
It's not worth it.

00:29:54.159 --> 00:29:57.195
This is the same thing
with X-ray specialists.

00:29:57.195 --> 00:30:01.099
Don't use n equals 1,
n equals 2, n equals 3.

00:30:01.099 --> 00:30:03.868
Use KLM, because
they prefer that.

00:30:03.868 --> 00:30:06.838
So the KLMs there are
simply the quantum numbers,

00:30:06.838 --> 00:30:08.573
the principle quantum numbers.

00:30:08.573 --> 00:30:16.014
So 1 is k, 2 is l, and 3 is m.

00:30:16.014 --> 00:30:18.283
And all we're doing is the
same kind of excitations

00:30:18.283 --> 00:30:20.585
as drawn there very well.

00:30:20.585 --> 00:30:23.021
So you see the incident
electron comes in,

00:30:23.021 --> 00:30:25.423
and now it's got
enough energy to excite

00:30:25.423 --> 00:30:27.826
one of those core electrons.

00:30:27.826 --> 00:30:30.628
It's got enough
energy to excite it.

00:30:30.628 --> 00:30:33.298
And so what it does is it trades
energy with that core electron.

00:30:33.298 --> 00:30:36.100
The core electron goes up,
and then it comes back down.

00:30:36.100 --> 00:30:39.170
Now, if we had,
say, an m level--

00:30:39.170 --> 00:30:43.842
so this is n equals 1, 2, 3.

00:30:43.842 --> 00:30:45.009
So if we had--

00:30:45.009 --> 00:30:46.010
now I can draw it.

00:30:46.010 --> 00:30:50.181
If I had some electron in my
k shell, my n equals 1 shell,

00:30:50.181 --> 00:30:55.086
and that electron got bumped
up to an l shell, well,

00:30:55.086 --> 00:30:58.890
now you know-- from our
work with the Bohr atom,

00:30:58.890 --> 00:31:00.525
you know that when
this goes back down,

00:31:00.525 --> 00:31:04.062
it's going to emit a photon.

00:31:04.062 --> 00:31:06.764
But this is very different now.

00:31:06.764 --> 00:31:08.199
This is very different.

00:31:08.199 --> 00:31:12.036
This photon has only that
energy, nothing in between.

00:31:12.036 --> 00:31:13.438
It's not continuous.

00:31:13.438 --> 00:31:14.806
I'm getting used
to wearing this.

00:31:14.806 --> 00:31:16.274
It feels good.

00:31:16.274 --> 00:31:17.475
It feels pretty good.

00:31:20.278 --> 00:31:23.581
So I could have
had an electron--

00:31:23.581 --> 00:31:26.384
instead of going to the l level,
maybe it went all the way up

00:31:26.384 --> 00:31:27.919
to the m level.

00:31:27.919 --> 00:31:31.389
And then from there,
it cascades back down,

00:31:31.389 --> 00:31:34.659
and it emits a photon.

00:31:34.659 --> 00:31:36.661
And the way this works
in terms of labeling

00:31:36.661 --> 00:31:42.700
is because it goes down to the
k level, these are k photons.

00:31:42.700 --> 00:31:44.302
And we go in order.

00:31:44.302 --> 00:31:46.571
So this is the least
energy k photon.

00:31:46.571 --> 00:31:47.872
It ended in k.

00:31:47.872 --> 00:31:57.448
So that's a k alpha or k--

00:31:57.448 --> 00:32:00.785
I'll just write it out
to be clear-- k beta.

00:32:00.785 --> 00:32:02.687
Oh, I hear sizzling.

00:32:02.687 --> 00:32:05.990
Oh, that's because
you're pouring.

00:32:05.990 --> 00:32:07.926
That's good.

00:32:07.926 --> 00:32:10.929
More liquid nitrogen
is never a bad thing.

00:32:10.929 --> 00:32:12.363
That's great.

00:32:12.363 --> 00:32:13.264
No, no, I'm good.

00:32:13.264 --> 00:32:14.599
You guys are doing such a good--

00:32:14.599 --> 00:32:16.834
now, OK.

00:32:16.834 --> 00:32:20.038
So if it's k beta-- because
it went to k, it ended at k.

00:32:20.038 --> 00:32:20.972
It gave off a photon.

00:32:20.972 --> 00:32:25.576
The photon is either
alpha, beta, gamma,

00:32:25.576 --> 00:32:26.544
depending on which one.

00:32:26.544 --> 00:32:30.615
But now here's the thing.

00:32:30.615 --> 00:32:36.321
You know if you go back
to that plot of energy--

00:32:36.321 --> 00:32:38.756
of lambda versus
intensity, well, you

00:32:38.756 --> 00:32:40.825
know that there's
nothing in between.

00:32:40.825 --> 00:32:43.394
There are no electronic
states in here.

00:32:43.394 --> 00:32:45.697
So you can't excite in between.

00:32:45.697 --> 00:32:47.632
This is forbidden.

00:32:47.632 --> 00:32:48.299
It's forbidden.

00:32:48.299 --> 00:32:52.070
So unlike the braking kind
of radiation, in this case,

00:32:52.070 --> 00:32:55.173
it's very, very discrete.

00:32:55.173 --> 00:32:57.675
And so now, if we plot
what you get from this,

00:32:57.675 --> 00:33:01.512
well, you're going to get a k
alpha peak that's very, very

00:33:01.512 --> 00:33:05.116
discrete, and you're going to
get a k beta peak that's only

00:33:05.116 --> 00:33:10.855
at the one energy
that is the difference

00:33:10.855 --> 00:33:14.592
between these levels,
k alpha k beta.

00:33:14.592 --> 00:33:15.626
So that's another kind.

00:33:15.626 --> 00:33:18.563
This is a type 2.

00:33:18.563 --> 00:33:23.368
This is second type of X-ray.

00:33:27.338 --> 00:33:29.574
OK.

00:33:29.574 --> 00:33:33.644
It sounded interesting.

00:33:33.644 --> 00:33:35.546
We'll let it keep going.

00:33:35.546 --> 00:33:37.115
We'll let it keep going.

00:33:37.115 --> 00:33:39.050
It's good because
it gives you a time.

00:33:39.050 --> 00:33:42.687
And I hope-- you guys
can just leave it.

