00:42:30.214 --> 00:42:32.449
Why is this important?

00:42:32.449 --> 00:42:34.718
This is important
because we will

00:42:34.718 --> 00:42:39.256
be shining x-rays as a source
of light onto crystals.

00:42:39.256 --> 00:42:41.892
And there's a very
important thing

00:42:41.892 --> 00:42:44.061
that happens when you
shine light onto something.

00:42:44.061 --> 00:42:45.296
And you can see it here.

00:42:45.296 --> 00:42:46.430
Light is a wave.

00:42:46.430 --> 00:42:47.798
Light is a particle.

00:42:47.798 --> 00:42:48.632
Light is a wave.

00:42:48.632 --> 00:42:49.500
Light is a particle.

00:42:49.500 --> 00:42:51.669
Have that discussion too.

00:42:51.669 --> 00:42:55.072
But here we're looking at it
as a wave, and waves diffract.

00:42:55.072 --> 00:42:59.243
That means that they can add
or cancel each other, right

00:42:59.243 --> 00:43:00.344
like water waves.

00:43:00.344 --> 00:43:02.046
But it depends on
the size of the thing

00:43:02.046 --> 00:43:03.113
they're interacting with.

00:43:03.113 --> 00:43:05.149
If the size of the thing
they're interacting with

00:43:05.149 --> 00:43:07.918
is the same as the
wavelength of light,

00:43:07.918 --> 00:43:10.454
then you can get a
diffraction pattern.

00:43:10.454 --> 00:43:13.724
And so when we
shine X-rays, which

00:43:13.724 --> 00:43:15.859
have wavelengths
that are the same

00:43:15.859 --> 00:43:20.297
as the spacing between
planes in a crystal,

00:43:20.297 --> 00:43:21.465
well, then it interacts.

00:43:21.465 --> 00:43:22.967
And it can diffract.

00:43:22.967 --> 00:43:27.037
And that allows us to determine
the structure of the crystal.

00:43:27.037 --> 00:43:29.139
That's called X-ray diffraction.

00:43:29.139 --> 00:43:31.575
And this is how it all started.

00:43:31.575 --> 00:43:34.445
It started with the
Bragg father and son

00:43:34.445 --> 00:43:36.747
pair, who actually
said in their paper

00:43:36.747 --> 00:43:40.951
the crystal is, in fact, acting
as a diffraction grating.

00:43:40.951 --> 00:43:44.388
And from that, you can literally
tell us which kind of crystal

00:43:44.388 --> 00:43:45.656
it is.

00:43:45.656 --> 00:43:48.726
I want to point
something out here.

00:43:48.726 --> 00:43:51.028
That's a father and son.

00:43:51.028 --> 00:43:54.732
They both won the Nobel Prize
together for this discovery.

00:43:54.732 --> 00:43:59.169
Now, I'm not putting
pressure on you guys.

00:43:59.169 --> 00:44:01.739
I'm just saying it's
not all about the kids.

00:44:01.739 --> 00:44:03.207
The parents got to step up too.

00:44:05.776 --> 00:44:06.944
So that's Bragg defraction.

00:44:06.944 --> 00:44:08.612
And that is one of the most--

00:44:08.612 --> 00:44:10.247
X-ray diffraction--
what we're going

00:44:10.247 --> 00:44:12.583
to do with these powerful
sources of light,

00:44:12.583 --> 00:44:14.151
this is one of the
most important ways

00:44:14.151 --> 00:44:16.920
to characterize
crystals that we have.

